<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<pretext>
    <article xml:id="worksheet">

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="wkst-mcmc">
    <title>Markov Chain Monte Carlo and Gerrymandering</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Modern mathematical approaches to detecting gerrymandering compare an enacted districting plan to a large collection of alternative plans satisfying the same legal constraints.
        Because the number of valid plans is astronomically large, we cannot list them all.
        Instead, we use <term>Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC)</term> to sample from this space.
      </p>
      <p>
        This worksheet explains the mathematics behind that idea using linear algebra and calculus.
      </p>
    </introduction>


      <p>
        Let <m>\Omega</m> be the set of all valid districting plans for a state.
        Each plan partitions precincts (or census blocks) into <m>k</m> contiguous districts with equal population.
      </p>

      <p>
        For each plan <m>P \in \Omega</m>, we compute a statistic
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        f : \Omega \to \mathbb{R}.
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        Examples include seat share, efficiency gap, or number of competitive districts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ideally, we would compute the expectation
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        \mathbb{E}[f] = \sum_{P \in \Omega} f(P)\,\pi(P),
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        where <m>\pi</m> is a probability distribution on <m>\Omega</m>.
        This is a discrete analogue of the integral
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        \int f(x)p(x)\,dx.
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        Since <m>\Omega</m> is too large to enumerate, we approximate this sum using MCMC.
      </p>

      <p>
        Suppose <m>\Omega = \{P_1,\dots,P_n\}</m>.
        A Markov chain on this space is described by a transition matrix
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        T = (t_{ij}),
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        where
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        t_{ij} = \Pr(P_i \to P_j).
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        The matrix <m>T</m> is stochastic: its entries are nonnegative and each row sums to 1.
      </p>

      <p>
        If <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is a probability vector describing a distribution on plans, then
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        \mathbf{v}_{new} = T\mathbf{v}.
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        A stationary distribution satisfies
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        T\boldsymbol{\pi} = \boldsymbol{\pi}.
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        Thus <m>\boldsymbol{\pi}</m> is an eigenvector of <m>T</m> with eigenvalue 1.
      </p>

      <p>
        Consider four precincts arranged in a line:
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        1 \text{ — } 2 \text{ — } 3 \text{ — } 4.
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        We divide them into two contiguous districts of two precincts each.
      </p>

      <p>
        There are exactly three valid districting plans:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><m>P_1 = \{1,2\} | \{3,4\}</m></li>
        <li><m>P_2 = \{1\} | \{2,3,4\}</m> (invalid: unequal population)</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        To keep populations equal, suppose each precinct has equal population.
        Then the valid plans are:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><m>P_1 = \{1,2\} | \{3,4\}</m></li>
        <li><m>P_2 = \{1,2,3\} | \{4\}</m> (invalid)</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        For simplicity, instead assume a cycle of four nodes:
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        1 - 2 - 3 - 4 - 1.
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        Now valid 2–2 partitions are:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><m>P_1 = \{1,2\} | \{3,4\}</m></li>
        <li><m>P_2 = \{2,3\} | \{4,1\}</m></li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        So <m>\Omega = \{P_1, P_2\}</m>.
      </p>

        <p>
          From either plan, with probability 1, we switch to the other plan.
          The transition matrix is
        </p>

        <md><mrow>
          T =
          \begin{pmatrix}
          0 \amp 1 \\
          1 \amp 0
          \end{pmatrix}.
        </mrow></md>

        <p>
          The stationary distribution satisfies
        </p>

        <md><mrow>
          T\boldsymbol{\pi} = \boldsymbol{\pi}.
        </mrow></md>

        <p>
          Solving,
        </p>

        <md><mrow>
          \boldsymbol{\pi} =
          \begin{pmatrix}
          1/2 \\
          1/2
          \end{pmatrix}.
        </mrow></md>

        <p>
          So in the long run, each plan appears half the time.
        </p>

        <p>
          Suppose under <m>P_1</m>, Party A wins 2 seats,
          and under <m>P_2</m>, Party A wins 1 seat.
        </p>

        <p>
          Define
        </p>

        <md><mrow>
          f(P_1) = 2, \quad f(P_2) = 1.
        </mrow></md>

        <p>
          The expected seat count under neutral sampling is
        </p>

        <md><mrow>
          \mathbb{E}[f] =
          \frac{1}{2}(2) + \frac{1}{2}(1)
          = \frac{3}{2}.
        </mrow></md>

        <p>
          If the enacted plan gives 2 seats, it lies above the ensemble mean.
        </p>

      <p>
        Instead of solving for <m>\boldsymbol{\pi}</m> directly (which is impossible in real states),
        we simulate the chain:
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        P_1, P_2, \dots, P_N.
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        Then approximate
      </p>

      <md><mrow>
        \mathbb{E}[f]
        \approx
        \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N f(P_i).
      </mrow></md>

      <p>
        This is Monte Carlo integration over the space of districting plans.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let <m>P^*</m> be the enacted plan.
        We compare <m>f(P^*)</m> to the distribution of <m>f(P_i)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        If <m>P^*</m> lies in the extreme tail of this distribution,
        it is statistical evidence that the enacted map is not typical of neutral plans.
      </p>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        MCMC transforms redistricting into a problem in linear algebra and numerical integration.
        We build a Markov chain on a huge combinatorial space,
        approximate its stationary distribution,
        and estimate expectations by averaging sampled plans.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
        </worksheet>
    </article>
</pretext>
